{# The GLOBAL STATE section, shared by every engine template that feeds the #}
{# global-state findings. Parameterized by (findings list, heading text, #}
{# scan coverage record) so each template passes its own heading prefix and #}
{# the coverage the producing scan stamped. The body is verbatim-pinned by #}
{# the section suite in src/lib.rs — wording or formatting drift fails CI. #}
{# The coverage record (issue #350) arrives as CoverageInfo strings: the #}
{# scan's status word and its admission line. The include appends exactly #}
{# one "Coverage:" qualification line — beside the entries, or as the whole #}
{# section beside the heading when the scan stamped no findings but admits #}
{# falling short — whenever the record carries an admission; a complete or #}
{# missing record renders exactly what it rendered before, and the line #}
{# names only bodies the scan never reached, never an examined-and-clean #}
{# body the record list left unmentioned. #}
{% macro global_state_section(findings, heading, coverage) %}
{% if !findings.is_empty() %}
## {{ heading }}

The program globals this function reads and writes, recorded by static
analysis. This function does not own this state — it shares it with the
rest of the binary. Give each global's storage a home in a struct the
translation threads through its units rather than in a Rust `static`, and
read or update it through that shared state instead of inventing a local
for it:

{% for finding in findings %}
- **{{ finding.suggestion|safe }}** ({{ finding.kind }} of `{{ finding.global }}`, confidence {{ finding.confidence }})
  Evidence: `{{ finding.evidence|safe }}`
{% endfor %}

{% endif %}
{% if !coverage.admission.is_empty() %}
{% if findings.is_empty() %}
## {{ heading }}

{% endif %}
Coverage: {{ coverage.admission }}

{% endif %}
{% endmacro %}
